// `crates/redox-ast/src/program/item/clause.rs`, expressed in Redox.
//
// Plan 48 (`docs/42 - 48 - Conformance and Self-Hosting.md`) leaf 2. Doc 02
// §4.8 phrases the conformance criterion as "the compiler's source can be
// expressed in Redox without the inline-Rust escape hatch", and this is one
// module of it: 45 lines of real compiler code, one of the 42 blocker-free
// files in `redox-ast`.
//
// What it proves: a real compiler data type is expressible — a declaration,
// named fields, derives, visibility, doc comments and verbatim types all
// survive. What it does not prove: that the compiler is expressible. This is
// 0.2% of it, and `docs/CONFORMANCE.md` counts the rest.
//
// No `rust """…"""` anywhere in this file, which is the whole point.
//
// Writing it found a defect. Every one of the four field doc comments below
// was **silently discarded** — gone from the `.xrs`, and deleted from this
// file by `redox fmt`. AGENTS.md calls that rule load-bearing: "comments are
// trivia, never discarded; they must reach the generated `.xrs`" (doc 02
// §10.1). The lexer classified a comment opening a continuation line as
// *trailing* the previous line, then held it for a `Newline` that a
// continuation never produces. No corpus file had a comment inside a
// continuation, so nothing caught it. Fixed in `redox-lexer`'s
// `at_line_start`, which is exactly the "hard way" doc 02 §4.8 said a port
// would find things.

use crate::Pattern;

use crate::program::item::clause_body::ClauseBody;

use redox_lexer::Span;

use redox_lexer::Trivia;

/// One equation of a function.
//
// Doc 18 §2.3. A function is a signature line plus **one or more** clauses,
// each a row of patterns and either a body or a list of guards:
//
// ```text
// fib :: u64 -> u64            tier :: u64 -> &'static str
// fib 0 = 0                    tier spend
// fib 1 = 1                      | spend > 10_000 = "platinum"
// fib n = fib (n - 1) + …        | otherwise      = "basic"
// ```
//
// Doc 03 spelled each clause as a closure with a repeated signature, which is
// why `RDX0045` existed to catch the clauses disagreeing. With the signature
// hoisted the disagreement reduces to arity, which `signature_from` reports.
#[derive(Debug, Clone, PartialEq, Eq)]
pub struct Clause {
    /// One pattern per parameter, in order.
    pub patterns: Vec<Pattern>,
    /// The clause's body: one block, or a guard ladder.
    pub body: ClauseBody,
    /// `where` locals — doc 18 §2.2.
    //
    // Emitted **before** the body, which is the only order Rust allows: a
    // `let` has to precede its use. Haskell's `where` reads the other way
    // round — the definition follows the expression that uses it — and that
    // reordering is the whole of the lowering.
    pub wheres: Vec<crate::Stmt>,
    pub span: Span,
    /// A comment that trailed the clause's last line.
    pub trailing: Option<Trivia>,
}
